== random graphs: DFS, intervals, classification, Lemma 6.1 ==
OK   DFS(v) from scratch marks exactly reach(v) (1200 random graphs, every start vertex)
OK   DFSAll calls DFS once per vertex and examines every edge once: O(V + E)
OK   the clock assigns pre and post the values 1, ..., 2V, each once
OK   active intervals are disjoint or nested
OK   [u.pre, u.post] contains [v.pre, v.post] iff u is an ancestor of v
OK   Lemma 6.1: (a) ancestor <=> (b) u.pre <= v.pre < v.post <= u.post <=> (c) u active just after DFS(v) is called <=> (d) path of new vertices from u to v just before DFS(u) is called
OK   the active vertices always form a directed path of tree edges ending at the current vertex
OK   no edge u->v has u.post < v.pre (the fourth ordering is impossible)
OK   classification by intervals = classification by the state of v when DFS(u) begins
OK   state of w when v->w is examined: new -> tree, active -> back, finished -> forward or cross
OK   number of tree edges = V - number of DFS trees
OK   u.post < v.post for an edge u->v iff it is a back edge, and then v reaches u
OK   wrapper with an added source s: same pre/post (shifted by 1), DFS trees = children of s
OK   a loop u->u is classified as a back edge (300 graphs with loops)
OK   path 0->1->2->3->4: DFSAll finds 1 tree in order 0..4 and 5 trees in order 4..0

== cycles and dags ==
OK   IsAcyclic (Figure 6.7) = no cycle by brute force (random vertex and edge orders, loops included)
OK   G has a cycle iff the DFS finds a back edge (any DFS)
OK   loop-free G has a cycle iff some edge u->v has u.post < v.post
OK   with loops allowed: G has a cycle iff some edge u->v has u.post <= v.post
OK   every nonempty dag has a source and a sink
OK   loop-free G is a dag iff every strong component is a single vertex

== topological sort ==
OK   for a dag, u.post > v.post for every edge u->v, so reverse postorder is a topological order (random orders)
OK   TopologicalSort (Figure 6.9, recursive call on w) writes S[V], ..., S[1] = reverse postorder, a topological order
OK   TopologicalSort fails gracefully (finds an active vertex) exactly on graphs with cycles
OK   as printed, TopSortDFS(v, clock) recurses on v forever as soon as it meets an edge to a new vertex (e.g. the one-edge dag a->b, a first)
OK   PostProcess and PostProcessDag call Process in postorder = reverse topological order
OK   topological orders of rev(G) = reversals of topological orders of G (150 small dags, all orders)
OK   every reverse topological order of a dag is a postorder (wrapper visits the vertices in that order)

== memoization is depth-first search; longest paths in dags ==
OK   memoized Edit(i, j) finalizes subproblems in exactly the postorder of a DFS of its dependency graph (200 random string pairs)
OK   the edit-distance dependency graph is a dag; row-major order is a reverse topological order
OK   edit distance = shortest path from (m, n) to (0, 0) in the dependency dag (weights 1, 1, [A[i] != B[j]])
OK   memoized LongestPath(s, t) = maximum over all s-to-t paths (brute force; 1500 dags, weights -5..9)
OK   dynamic-programming LongestPath (postorder of DFSAll) = brute force; every w.LLP read is already computed
OK   LLP(v) = -infinity exactly for the vertices that cannot reach t
OK   as printed (return inside the if), LongestPath returns nothing on its second call on c in the diamond s->a->c, s->b->c, c->t; corrected it returns 3

== strong components ==
OK   strong component of v = reach(v) ∩ reach^-1(v), reach^-1 by searching rev(G)
OK   scc(G) is a dag (1200 random graphs)
OK   rev(scc(G)) = scc(rev(G))
OK   u reaches v in G iff S(u) reaches S(v) in scc(G)
OK   Lemma 6.2: each strong component has exactly one vertex without a parent in it — its earliest-starting vertex, an ancestor of all of it
OK   tree paths from the root of a component to its vertices stay inside the component
OK   Lemma 6.3: the last vertex of any postordering of G lies in a source component
OK   the last vertex of a postordering of rev(G) lies in a sink component of G
OK   edge of G from component C to C': in the DFS of rev(G), some vertex of C' finishes after all of C
OK   KosarajuSharir (Figure 6.16) labels every vertex with a vertex of its own strong component, equal labels iff same component
OK   Kosaraju-Sharir: each LabelOneDFS call from phase 2 labels exactly one strong component
OK   Kosaraju-Sharir finds the components sinks first (a reverse topological order of scc(G))
OK   FindLow (Figure 6.18) computes low(v) as defined (min start time over tree paths + one non-tree edge)
OK   Lemma 6.4: v is the root of a sink component iff low(v) = v.pre and low(w) < w.pre for every proper descendant w
OK   Tarjan (Figure 6.19) labels every vertex with the earliest-starting vertex of its component
OK   Tarjan emits the components in reverse topological order of scc(G); the first one is a sink
OK   Tarjan's first component need not be a source component (so 'source' on book p. 242 should be 'sink')
OK   when v.low = v.pre, popping down to v pops exactly v's component, v deepest
OK   the almost-algorithm of Figure 6.15 (sink vertex by magic) labels the strong components
OK   a->b, b->a, a->c from a with b first: postorder b c a; b's component {a, b} is not a sink (edge a->c)
OK   Kosaraju-Sharir with a breadth-first second phase gives the same components
OK   symmetric Kosaraju-Sharir (phase 1 on G, phase 2 on rev(G)) gives the same components
OK   vertices ordered by a topological order of scc(G): every backward edge joins two vertices of one component
OK   G strongly connected iff one search of G and one of rev(G) from the same vertex reach everything

